ok   1.x value-based: np.float32(1) + 1.0 -> float64 (scalar + scalar promotes to float64)  (got float64)
ok   1.x value-based: np.int8(100) + 100 -> int64 200, no warning  (got 200 int64, warnings [])
ok   1.x value-based: uint8 array + 300 -> uint16 [301]  (got uint16)
ok   1.x value-based: np.uint8(1) + 300 -> int64 301
ok   1.x value-based: np.float32(1) + 3e100 -> float64 3e100
ok   1.x value-based: float32 array + np.float64(1) -> float32 (0-d operands ignored for the dtype)
ok   1.x value-based: uint8 array + np.int64(1) -> uint8; uint8 array + 200 -> uint8 (wraps to 44)
ok   1.x value-based: np.array(1.0, float32) + 1e-14 == 1.0 is False (0-d array computed in float64)
ok   1.x value-based: (np.float32(1) + 1j) is complex128
ok   promote_types over all 196 pairs of bool/int/uint/float/complex equals the documented rules (uint64+int64 -> float64, int16+float16 -> float32, int32+float32 -> float64, float64+complex64 -> complex128) and result_type of arrays agrees  (mismatches [])
ok   documented examples: promote_types(int8, uint8) = int16, (int16, uint16) = int32, (int64, uint64) = float64, (int8, float16) = float16, (uint8, float16) = float16, (int16, float16) = float32, (float16, complex64) = complex64
ok   promote_types is symmetric (all pairs)
   promote_types is NOT associative for 28 of 2744 triples, e.g. [('int8', 'uint8', 'float16', 'float32', 'float16'), ('int8', 'uint16', 'float16', 'float64', 'float32'), ('int8', 'uint16', 'float32', 'float64', 'float32')] (documented nowhere; result_type with several dtypes is NOT the left-to-right pairwise reduction either, see next line)
   result_type(int8, uint8, float16) = float16 (pairwise left-to-right would give float32); result_type(int8, float16, uint8) = float16; result_type(int8, uint16, float16) = float32
ok   result_type with three dtypes is order-independent for (int8, uint8, float16) (recorded: it is not the naive left-to-right pairwise reduction)
ok   can_cast(a, b, 'safe') over all pairs equals promote_types(a, b) == b (the safe cast lattice)  (mismatches [])
ok   can_cast specifics: int64->float64 True (documented safe though lossy), uint64->int64 False, float64->float32 False, int16->float16 False, int8->float16 True, float32->complex64 True, float64->complex64 False
ok   can_cast casting modes: 'no' only identical, 'equiv' same-kind/size, 'same_kind' float64->float32 True int->float True float->int False, 'unsafe' everything
ok   can_cast with a Python int value is value-based on 1.x: can_cast(300, uint8) False, can_cast(100, uint8) True
ok   result_type of an int8 array with a Python float is float64 (2.x, weak float -> default) / float64 (1.x)
ok   result_type(int8 array, Python int 1) is int8 on both; result_type(np.int8, np.float32) is float32
ok   np.add(int8 array, int16 array).dtype is int16; (uint8 + int8) arrays give int16; (uint64 + int64) arrays give float64
